::STAT_U02_L04_GP_Q01::
Question ID: STAT_U02_L04_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.4
Question:
A distribution has a long tail toward larger values. What is its shape?
{
=A. Skewed right#Correct. A right-skewed distribution has a tail toward larger values.
~B. Skewed left#Skewed left means the tail extends toward smaller values.
~C. Symmetric#Symmetric distributions are balanced.
~D. Uniform#Uniform distributions have roughly equal frequencies across values.
}

::STAT_U02_L04_GP_Q02::
Question ID: STAT_U02_L04_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.4
Question:
A distribution is balanced on both sides of the center. How should it be described?
{
~A. Skewed right#Right skew has a long tail toward larger values.
=B. Symmetric#Correct. Symmetric distributions are balanced around the center.
~C. Skewed left#Left skew has a long tail toward smaller values.
~D. Clustered only#A cluster is a group of values, not the overall balance description.
}

::STAT_U02_L04_GP_Q03::
Question ID: STAT_U02_L04_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.4
Question:
In 4, 5, 5, 6, 7, 22, where is the cluster?
{
~A. At 22 only#22 is a possible outlier, not the cluster.
~B. Between 7 and 22#There is a gap more than a cluster there.
=C. Between 4 and 7#Correct. Most values are grouped from 4 through 7.
~D. There is no cluster#Most values are close together from 4 to 7.
}

::STAT_U02_L04_GP_Q04::
Question ID: STAT_U02_L04_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.4
Question:
Which feature is an interval with no data values?
{
~A. Cluster#A cluster is where many values group together.
~B. Peak#A peak is a high frequency area.
~C. Outlier#An outlier is an unusually far value.
=D. Gap#Correct. A gap is a space in the distribution with no data values.
}

::STAT_U02_L04_GP_Q05::
Question ID: STAT_U02_L04_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.4
Question:
Why might the median be preferred for a skewed distribution?
{
=A. The median is less affected by the tail#Correct. Skew and outliers can pull the mean, while the median is resistant.
~B. The median always equals the range#Median and range are different summaries.
~C. The median measures spread#Median measures center.
~D. The median requires no ordered data#Finding the median requires ordering values.
}

